<main id="main" class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head">
    <h2>Income &amp; MAGI</h2>
    <p class="lede">Three income streams, one measuring stick. For Medi-Cal and Covered California, everything is measured by household MAGI &mdash; and the 2027 work rule's $580 standard is <em>itself</em> measured with MAGI-based monthly income (42 CFR 435.552(f)), so unearned income from any source can satisfy it in a qualifying month. The federal tax character of your trading gains (section 1256 or otherwise) does not change either answer &mdash; and IRS rules explain why full-time trading plus self-managed rentals still produce $0 <em>earned</em> (self-employment) income.</p>
  </div>

  <section aria-labelledby="streams-h">
    <h3 id="streams-h">Your three income streams</h3>
    {{TABLE:income-treatment.csv::Income treatment for MAGI, taxes, and the work requirement}}
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="magi-h">
    <h3 id="magi-h">The MAGI math</h3>
    <div class="panel blue">
      <p><strong>MAGI = adjusted gross income + untaxed foreign income + non-taxable Social Security benefits + tax-exempt interest</strong> (SSI excluded). AGI already includes your taxable interest, net capital gains, and net rental income.</p>
      <p>For your situation, the practical formula is:</p>
      <p><code>MAGI &asymp; net rental income (Schedule E) + savings interest + net trading gains (after capital-loss offsets, up to $3,000 against other income) + any other taxable income</code></p>
      <p>Consequences worth planning around:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A big trading year can push MAGI over the Medi-Cal limit (138% FPL) or over the 400% FPL subsidy cliff &mdash; the marketplace would then be the path, possibly with no subsidy.</li>
        <li>A loss year (rental losses limited by passive-activity rules; trading losses netted against gains) can drop MAGI back under the Medi-Cal limit.</li>
        <li>Household means the tax household: you, a spouse if filing jointly, and tax dependents (with income of dependents who aren't required to file excluded).</li>
        <li>From January 1, 2027, if you are in Medi-Cal's expansion group: one month with MAGI-based income &ge; $580 satisfies the work rule for that renewal period in California (new applicants: the month before applying). Medi-Cal budgets MAGI monthly and counts a taxable lump sum only in the month received, while Covered California uses estimated <em>annual</em> MAGI &mdash; volatile trading income can therefore look different to the two programs; keep records month by month.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="kalshi-h">
    <h3 id="kalshi-h">Kalshi specifics</h3>
    <div class="panel warn">
      <p><strong>What Kalshi itself reports (official company documentation):</strong> Kalshi is a CFTC-regulated Designated Contract Market. It issues <strong>1099-INT</strong> (interest payments), <strong>1099-MISC</strong> (credits/rewards), <strong>1099-B</strong> (proceeds from broker transactions involving crypto transfers), and <strong>1099-DA</strong> (digital-asset transactions through ZeroHash, computed FIFO), plus a monthly-updated Profit &amp; Loss statement. It does not issue a comprehensive 1099 covering event-contract trading profit and loss, and states its information is not tax advice.</p>
      <p><strong>The open tax question (flagged):</strong> whether Kalshi event contracts qualify as <em>section 1256 contracts</em> (60% long-term / 40% short-term capital gain, year-end mark-to-market, Form 6781) has no IRS ruling either way; practitioners disagree and Kalshi takes no position. The Bipartisan Policy Center documented the uncertainty in August 2026. <strong>For healthcare eligibility this question does not matter</strong>: under any characterization, trading gains are unearned investment income that lands in AGI and therefore MAGI.</p>
      <p><strong>Trading hours as &ldquo;work&rdquo; (flagged):</strong> federal Medicaid &ldquo;work&rdquo; includes paid, in-kind, and unpaid work, with self-employment included per the CMS preamble and DHCS guidance &mdash; but no official source addresses hours spent trading your own account, and IRS law says own-account trading gains are not self-employment <em>earnings</em> even for a qualifying full-time trader (<a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc429" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS Topic 429</a>). That tax rule validates reporting $0 earned income; it does not settle the Medicaid hours question. Verified no-earned-income alternatives: the $580 MAGI-based income month, 80 hours of structured community service, or at least half-time school. Get written county confirmation before relying on trading hours.</p>
      <p><strong>Which test uses which number.</strong> For both Medi-Cal/marketplace financial eligibility and the 2027 work rule's $580/month income route, controlling federal regulation (<a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-435/section-435.552" target="_blank" rel="noopener">42 CFR &sect;435.552(f)(2)</a>) and California statute (<a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260AB2161" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WIC &sect;14005.69(e)(6)</a>, as amended by AB 2161) specify <em>MAGI-based monthly income</em> (which counts <em>net</em> Schedule E rental and royalty profit, taxable and tax-exempt interest, and net capital/trading gains). Earlier preliminary county guidance (<a href="https://www.dhcs.ca.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/25-30.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DHCS ACWDL 25-30</a>, Dec. 2025) phrased the $580 standard as an individual's &ldquo;total gross income for a month,&rdquo; and month-by-month county documentation for realized trading gains and multi-member household allocation remain open &mdash; see FL-06 and FL-17 on <a href="flagged-for-review.html">Flagged for Review</a>.</p>
      <p class="kbd-note">Sources: <a href="https://help.kalshi.com/en/articles/13823849-what-tax-documentation-does-kalshi-provide" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kalshi Help Center &mdash; Tax Documentation</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-access/f6781_accessible.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS Form 6781</a>; <a href="https://bipartisanpolicy.org/issue-brief/unpacking-tax-uncertainties-for-prediction-markets/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bipartisan Policy Center issue brief (secondary)</a>; <a href="https://www.dhcs.ca.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/25-30.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DHCS ACWDL 25-30</a>. See <a href="flagged-for-review.html">Flagged for Review</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="rental-h">
    <h3 id="rental-h">Rental income and the &ldquo;property manager&rdquo; question</h3>
    <div class="panel">
      <ul>
        <li>Net rental income (rents minus expenses like mortgage interest, taxes, insurance, repairs, depreciation) is what lands in AGI and MAGI &mdash; not gross rents.</li>
        <li>Rental real estate is generally a <strong>passive activity</strong>: losses are limited, though up to $25,000 of rental loss can offset other income if you actively participate (the allowance phases out at higher income), and qualifying <strong>real estate professionals</strong> are exempt from the passive limits.</li>
        <li><strong>If you are paid fees to manage property for other people, that is self-employment income (earned income)</strong> &mdash; and providing substantial services primarily for tenants' convenience moves rental reporting to Schedule C. Managing <em>your own</em> rentals is reported as rental income, which is generally <em>not</em> self-employment earnings. With $0 earned income, this research assumes the self-managed, no-third-party-fees case &mdash; if you collect management fees, report them as earned business income, after which the $0-earned-income premise no longer holds.</li>
        <li>Whether documented hours managing your own units count as Medicaid &ldquo;work&rdquo; toward 80 hours is unresolved (FL-12) &mdash; do not assume; the verified path on rental cash flow alone is the $580 MAGI-based income month (using <em>net</em> rental income, so a depreciation-driven Schedule E loss month may not clear it even if gross rent does).</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="kbd-note">Sources: <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS Publication 527</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc414" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS Topic 414</a>; <a href="https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i1040se" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IRS Schedule E instructions</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
